## Finding
The standalone Palworld acceptance probe is not part of `SharedWorlds.sln`, and the Palworld read-only workflow only ran the adapter test project. Therefore #194's source change to `tools/SharedWorlds.PalworldProbe/Program.cs` was not actually compiled by CI.

The probe also intentionally consumes adapter-internal acceptance seams (`PalworldInstallationDiscovery`, REST acceptance/configuration/client types), while the adapter assembly only exposed internals to its test assembly.

## Smallest build-boundary fix
- explicitly friend `SharedWorlds.PalworldProbe` from the Palworld adapter assembly;
- make Palworld read-only CI build `tools/SharedWorlds.PalworldProbe/SharedWorlds.PalworldProbe.csproj` in Release before adapter tests;
- upload the probe build log alongside the adapter test log.

This does not add the probe to the product package or solution, does not expose adapter internals publicly, and does not change Palworld runtime/product behavior. It only ensures the acceptance executable cannot silently rot outside CI.

Scope against #194: exactly two files:
- `src/SharedWorlds.GameAdapters/Palworld/Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs`: +1;
- `.github/workflows/palworld-plm-probe-ci.yml`: CI build/log step only.

## Qualification
Exact head `9f43029221b1a011a86f0a35bf96073cdda2b61c` is fully green across:
- CI;
- Windows acceptance package;
- Palworld read-only CI;
- 7 Days to Die adapter CI;
- Project Zomboid adapter CI.

The decisive new `Build Palworld probe` step completed successfully before the Palworld adapter tests, so the standalone acceptance executable is now explicitly compiled in CI.

